0.9613969874278565 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428506 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1031 "The Image and Rank of a Linear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1033 1963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049539 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fcfd540b-2f04-5491-bc34-5138611f81d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429129 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429130 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429131 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1031 "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1033 3645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049895 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1031 "The Kernel of a Linear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1033 1960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049903 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1cfff0a4-a88a-5e9d-8bad-9c1429faff51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173866 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dff6c919-be8a-5f13-bc6a-323d7a217ae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175719 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175719 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of these transformations map $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line?\n\n![](images/89/8914e3b11b6ccb726df2363fbc238d4c87ab6948e217ea54617e574a4babc4f2.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "954c2d7b-19d8-584b-aeb5-0927c79e2a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175719 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear transformation over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $T_1$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of $T_1$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}(T_1)=1.$\n\n- $T_2$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Indeed, $T_2$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_2)=2.$\n\n- $T_3$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ since $T_3$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T_1$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175720 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175720 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n![](images/bf/bfab47037972e5cbb408473b3c94463b8100d622971ed66ba5dabc9ff15be7bb.svg)\nFind the equation of the line that the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91058f47-ebdb-5a02-ba3d-de01a1912041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175720 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1$ and $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(-9,-3),$ which are defined by the columns of $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-3-2}{-9-6} = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=\\dfrac13x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175721 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175721 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=-5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$\n\nII. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\Bbb R^2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd98751e-7fbd-5afa-b7ad-725ec479c996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175721 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(T) = \\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(-1,5)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=-5x$ since $5=-5\\cdot (-1).$ Hence, \n\n$$\n\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\Bbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1039 "Identitying the Type of the Image of a Linear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176087 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b1fb22de-94c2-51a9-803b-f2bd77bcb012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the One-Dimensional Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176088 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "390ecc8f-2938-5466-b49b-964a67f79755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1045 "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fb65b447-b251-5b8b-84fb-25df81ffc211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto the whole of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c56c149d-6734-5355-8f48-32ec1a8b06b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176268 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{1}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=2.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{2}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{3}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477614 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477615 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477616 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n- **II.** $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4acbb26f-7e4b-53ce-a37d-bd74763516c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1061 70373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79dd6a5b-b38e-5027-95bc-e8b46a7f3a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1061 70374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9da32ac-2fba-5b76-a770-d303f55e3aff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1061 70377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad2e7f46-d633-5065-9a91-a94b5e3bcf75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1061 70380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51ed8d83-4d25-56a7-994d-262c6c456b9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the following linear transformation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, it can only be one of the following:  \n\n- the whole of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$\n\n- a line through the origin, or\n\n- a single point (vector), namely the origin $(0,0).$\n\nLet's denote the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ by $T.$ It turns out that the dimension of the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ depends on $\\text{rank}(T)$ (or equivalently on $\\text{nullity}(T)$ by the rank-nullity theorem). Indeed, we have the following:\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=2,$ then the two columns of $T$ are linearly independent. This means $T$ is invertible and therefore, $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) =  \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto  $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7d/7de193042587cb9362641cbb541477a5e4951e96d9e82f074fb0cc3a86a80d3a.svg)\n\n![](images/88/88baa47cae14d4a43d59b366c13b3c29376678edef2fe34ff505a942ef6af986.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=1,$ then there is a nonzero vector that generates the image of $\\mathbf{T}.$ In this case, the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a one-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^2$. So, it is a line passing through the origin.\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=2x\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/49/4952615b0c4ddf85cc6d60483f8c0587093c67734a320cde913644fe8fdafb55.svg)\nNotice that the points $(2,4)$ and $(1,2),$ defined by the columns of $T,$ lie on the line $y=2x.$\n\n![](images/03/0322f183c16fe82515277d10cf27a93ae753f8eadf7eeb1db4d3c5e404480b09.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=0$ then,  according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that  $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{0}$ for all $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Bbb R^2$. So, in this case, we have that\n![](images/4a/4a0ce67cffc02e28edffb9efeedca2e21ffc7c0d8b72ccfa0566cb29f6679865.svg)\nwhich maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\{(0,0)\\},$ i.e., the set containing only the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/12/1252907a1e9767201dd326945f328f91fc493ea283b244dbfee42a7c4248d9e6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1065 8344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ec0551e-64f7-52a7-a0e7-827e6a8944f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto a line?\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d90171a1-3d85-53bd-8f39-df0500121339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364824 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{1}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{2}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{3}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364825 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4d4cdb48-2c70-5c58-8179-52d11b0057e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364826 #edb/ref 1080 "${T}_{2}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364826 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364826 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6707aa95-7385-5666-ab9f-8e517cb70b8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364827 #edb/ref 1080 "${T}_{1}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364827 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364827 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1b4b4fb-b2a1-5812-abfe-7f88606e3432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364828 #edb/ref 1080 "${T}_{3}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e6543ba-9d3b-5c31-8a43-6c7e29ccd78f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364829 #edb/ref 1080 "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17